{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"agent_message","text":"Preflight passes with the Candidate.\n\n{\"submission\": \"submission.json\", \"summary\": \"Second turn on the SwiftPaxos/EPaxos replica. The first map/feedback was accepted (audit_spec_version 1, understanding usable), so this turn focused the recovery path. New sourced result: the defer-cycle guard in handleTryPreAcceptReply is inert — lb.possibleQuorum is initialized all-false and only ever assigned false (epaxos/epaxos.go:1445-1446), and the notInQuorum loop tests the constant lb.possibleQuorum[tpar.AcceptorId] for every q instead of lb.possibleQuorum[q] (epaxos/epaxos.go:1461), so notInQuorum is always r.N and the comparison notInQuorum == r.N/2 cannot hold; the block introduced by the in-source comment 'this is to prevent defer cycles' never runs. I also re-investigated the handlePreAccept branch that writes r.InstanceSpace[preAccept.LeaderId][preAccept.Instance].Cmds: every path that sets an instance to ACCEPTED/COMMITTED also sets Cmds from the same bookkeeping, so no history yielding Status>=ACCEPTED with Cmds==nil could be sourced; it is retained as a local anomaly, not raised as a defect. Submitted action=continue with one new Candidate (disposition concrete_suspicion, priority 1) whose question is whether the TryPreAccept defer-cycle guard can fire and whether a deferred recovery can stall, principal fact F-RECOVERY-DECISION, direct behavior B-TPA-REPLY-LEADER, supporting B-RECOVERY-START / B-PREPARE-REPLY-LEADER / B-EXEC, with counterevidence that sub-case 4's predicate duplicates sub-case 3 (so TryPreAccept may be unreachable) and that executeCommands may retrigger recovery. No defect or obligation is claimed; preflight validation returns valid=true and formal acceptance/execution remain the controller's.\"}"}}
